      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
      "created_at": "2013-07-11T20:25:04.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"TCP\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/tcp/48/2549_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> TCP:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>TheJayMann wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;Pydro wrote:<br><blockquote>4. It is all from 1 attack though. not multiple attacks. Even if an attack does 0 damage, it would still use it up.&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nActually, if something does damage which ends up with a total of 0 (or less) then no damage is taken, thus it doesn't qualify as next damage (thus an attack of 0 will not remove stealth protection)However, irreducible damage would use up the protection, with none of the benifets of the protection.\n</blockquote></div>\nThat seems wrong to me. Wouldn't that make it possible for Wraith to become completely unkillable against certain villains without irreducible damage?\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Not sure how you reached that conclusion. I think JayMann is saying that if an attack is reduced to zero damage by something OTHER than the stealth power, than the stealth reduction doesn't kick in.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Mind you, assuming I'm correct about what JayMann wrote, I disagree. Even if someone tried to somehow attack The Wraith with a negative 50 million attack, her Stealth power kicks in on the next attack. Even if she's immune, that reduction kicks in on the next attack.</p>",
